From: Protector of Earth Recomp
Subject: [PATCH] VU1 interpreter: the FMAC model is not forced inline on MSVC

What broke:
  With patch 0020 the FMAC result and flag model (normalizeFmacResult,
  calculateFmacExactResult, updateFmacFlags, applyFmacDest, ...) is forced
  inline, so translated code with a constant upper word folds through it.
  GCC and Clang drop the opcode cases a constant word cannot reach before
  inlining. MSVC inlines first: every one of the ~70 cases of execUpper
  received the whole model, for each function of translated code. One
  generated file of the Ben 10 port (about 50 upper functions and 210
  pairs) made cl.exe pass 31 GB of committed memory alone and was stopped;
  a parallel build failed with C1002 (out of heap space in pass 2) and
  froze the machine.

The change:
  - PS2_VU_FMAC_INLINE: PS2_VU_FORCEINLINE on GCC and Clang (unchanged),
    plain inline on MSVC, which then decides by its own limits.
  - The seven model functions use it; the small helpers (broadcast,
    normalizeResult, applyDest, applyDestAcc) stay forced inline.
  Same code on GCC and Clang; on MSVC the same results, the model inlined
  where the compiler chooses.

--- a/ps2xRuntime/include/runtime/ps2_vu1.h
+++ b/ps2xRuntime/include/runtime/ps2_vu1.h
@@ -20,6 +20,18 @@
 #define PS2_VU_FORCEINLINE inline __attribute__((always_inline))
 #endif
 
+// The FMAC result and flag model (runtime/ps2_vu1_fmac_inl.h): forced inline
+// on GCC and Clang, which drop the unused opcode cases of a constant upper
+// word before inlining. MSVC inlines first and prunes afterwards: forcing
+// the model into every case of the upper executor made one file of
+// translated code need more than 30 GB to compile, so there the compiler
+// decides.
+#if defined(_MSC_VER)
+#define PS2_VU_FMAC_INLINE inline
+#else
+#define PS2_VU_FMAC_INLINE PS2_VU_FORCEINLINE
+#endif
+
 struct VU1State
 {
     float vf[32][4];
